1
00:00:00,333 --> 00:00:05,173
For years, the race to build better AI looked almost embarrassingly simple.

2
00:00:05,800 --> 00:00:08,774
Want a smarter model? Buy more GPUs.

3
00:00:09,200 --> 00:00:12,546
Want it smarter still? Build a bigger data centre.

4
00:00:12,546 --> 00:00:13,662
Spend more money.

5
00:00:14,067 --> 00:00:18,013
Better intelligence, it seemed, was mostly a shopping problem.

6
00:00:20,132 --> 00:00:21,626
Then the rules changed.

7
00:00:22,432 --> 00:00:28,980
In October 2022, the United States restricted exports of the most powerful

8
00:00:28,980 --> 00:00:33,891
AI chips, like NVIDIA's A100 and H100, to China.

9
00:00:34,299 --> 00:00:39,173
NVIDIA answered with China versions, the A800 and the H800.

10
00:00:39,173 --> 00:00:44,047
Nearly the same chips, but with slower connections between them.

11
00:00:44,465 --> 00:00:50,454
A year later, in October 2023, the rules were tightened again, and

12
00:00:50,454 --> 00:00:52,949
the H800 itself was restricted.

13
00:00:53,365 --> 00:00:59,386
And by April 2025, even the H20, a chip built specifically for

14
00:00:59,386 --> 00:01:03,400
the Chinese market, needed a US export licence.

15
00:01:05,532 --> 00:01:09,930
In the middle of all this, a Chinese lab called DeepSeek published

16
00:01:09,930 --> 00:01:11,763
a model called DeepSeek V3.

17
00:01:12,065 --> 00:01:16,925
They reported training it on a cluster of 2,048 H800s, the very

18
00:01:16,925 --> 00:01:21,380
chip that had been designed to sit just under the line.

19
00:01:21,799 --> 00:01:26,976
The full training run, they said, took about 2.8 million GPU hours.

20
00:01:26,976 --> 00:01:32,152
And, by their own benchmarks, the model it produced was comparable to

21
00:01:32,152 --> 00:01:33,447
leading closed models.

22
00:01:34,065 --> 00:01:36,337
People called it impossible. It wasn't.

23
00:01:36,337 --> 00:01:40,881
But it was surprising. And the reason it was surprising is the

24
00:01:40,881 --> 00:01:41,639
whole story.

25
00:01:42,465 --> 00:01:45,594
Because the real question isn't how many chips they had.

26
00:01:45,594 --> 00:01:49,349
It's this. What do you do when you can't simply buy your

27
00:01:49,349 --> 00:01:50,913
way out of a problem?

28
00:01:51,932 --> 00:01:56,715
Less than two years later, DeepSeek published another paper, about a different

29
00:01:56,715 --> 00:02:01,099
wall entirely. Memory. They got one number down to 890 bytes.

30
00:02:01,099 --> 00:02:05,881
By the end of this video, you'll know exactly what that number

31
00:02:05,881 --> 00:02:07,874
measures, and why it matters.

32
00:02:08,499 --> 00:02:12,334
But to get there, we have to start at the very bottom.

33
00:02:12,334 --> 00:02:13,613
With a single multiplication.

34
00:02:15,465 --> 00:02:19,261
Here is the most important operation in all of modern AI.

35
00:02:19,765 --> 00:02:22,725
Three times four. Twelve. That's it.

36
00:02:22,725 --> 00:02:23,712
One multiplication.

37
00:02:24,332 --> 00:02:26,405
Now let's give those numbers jobs.

38
00:02:26,405 --> 00:02:30,550
Suppose the three is a piece of information coming in, an input.

39
00:02:30,550 --> 00:02:34,004
And the four is how much we care about it.

40
00:02:34,004 --> 00:02:34,695
A weight.

41
00:02:35,099 --> 00:02:39,943
A neuron, in an artificial neural network, is barely more than this.

42
00:02:39,943 --> 00:02:44,788
It takes several inputs, multiplies each one by its own weight, and

43
00:02:44,788 --> 00:02:45,999
adds everything up.

44
00:02:46,399 --> 00:02:50,673
Those weights are what people mean when they say a model has

45
00:02:50,673 --> 00:02:54,591
parameters. Every parameter is just one number, one of these weights.

46
00:02:56,399 --> 00:03:00,560
Line the inputs up in a column, line the weights up in

47
00:03:00,560 --> 00:03:04,028
a row, and this whole neuron becomes one tidy operation.

48
00:03:04,432 --> 00:03:07,431
Multiply the first pair. The second pair.

49
00:03:07,431 --> 00:03:11,715
The third. Then add. Mathematicians call it a dot product.

50
00:03:12,132 --> 00:03:16,471
Notice what it cost. Three multiplications, and a few additions.

51
00:03:16,471 --> 00:03:21,678
People lump a multiply and an add together and count them as

52
00:03:21,678 --> 00:03:23,413
floating point operations. FLOPs.

53
00:03:23,832 --> 00:03:25,301
Hold on to that word.

54
00:03:25,301 --> 00:03:28,534
Everything in this story is going to be measured in it.

55
00:03:30,363 --> 00:03:32,711
A real layer doesn't have one neuron.

56
00:03:32,711 --> 00:03:35,730
It has many, all looking at the same inputs.

57
00:03:35,730 --> 00:03:39,755
Stack their weight rows on top of each other, and you get

58
00:03:39,755 --> 00:03:40,426
a matrix.

59
00:03:40,830 --> 00:03:45,688
Each row meets the input, does its dot product, and produces one

60
00:03:45,688 --> 00:03:47,712
output. Row one, output one.

61
00:03:47,712 --> 00:03:49,331
Row two, output two.

62
00:03:49,630 --> 00:03:54,098
And if we feed in three inputs at once, three tokens say,

63
00:03:54,098 --> 00:03:56,704
it becomes a matrix times a matrix.

64
00:03:56,704 --> 00:03:58,566
Every row meets every column.

65
00:03:58,897 --> 00:04:02,702
Three rows, three columns, three multiplications each.

66
00:04:02,702 --> 00:04:07,593
Twenty-seven multiplications, for the smallest matrix you can draw.

67
00:04:09,597 --> 00:04:17,066
Now the real thing. Inside DeepSeek V3, each token is represented by

68
00:04:17,066 --> 00:04:18,311
7,168 numbers.

69
00:04:18,730 --> 00:04:24,489
Pushing one token through a single square matrix of that size takes

70
00:04:24,489 --> 00:04:27,369
about 51 million multiplications. One token.

71
00:04:27,369 --> 00:04:28,329
One matrix.

72
00:04:28,830 --> 00:04:32,921
A model is hundreds of these matrices, stacked in layers.

73
00:04:32,921 --> 00:04:37,830
A useful rule of thumb: running a model costs about two operations

74
00:04:37,830 --> 00:04:42,738
for every parameter it uses, for every token it reads or writes.

75
00:04:43,163 --> 00:04:49,631
For V3, with its 37 billion active parameters, that's roughly 74 billion

76
00:04:49,631 --> 00:04:53,943
operations. For every single word fragment it produces.

77
00:04:54,563 --> 00:04:57,078
So that's what compute is.

78
00:04:57,078 --> 00:04:59,594
Not magic. Not thinking, exactly.

79
00:04:59,594 --> 00:05:04,121
Multiplication and addition, repeated an astronomical number of times.

80
00:05:04,530 --> 00:05:06,356
Which raises an obvious question.

81
00:05:06,356 --> 00:05:09,641
What kind of machine can do that fast enough?

82
00:05:11,197 --> 00:05:15,718
A normal processor, a CPU, is built like a few brilliant specialists.

83
00:05:15,718 --> 00:05:20,239
Each one is fast and clever, but there are only a handful

84
00:05:20,239 --> 00:05:20,993
of them.

85
00:05:21,297 --> 00:05:25,278
Give them a matrix multiplication, and they mostly work through it a

86
00:05:25,278 --> 00:05:26,605
chunk at a time.

87
00:05:26,930 --> 00:05:30,129
But look at what a matrix multiplication actually is.

88
00:05:30,129 --> 00:05:33,327
Every output cell is its own little dot product.

89
00:05:33,327 --> 00:05:36,525
None of them needs to wait for any other.

90
00:05:36,930 --> 00:05:40,982
A graphics processor, a GPU, bets everything on that fact.

91
00:05:40,982 --> 00:05:45,844
Instead of a few clever cores, it has thousands of simple ones,

92
00:05:45,844 --> 00:05:49,491
all doing multiply and add at the same time.

93
00:05:49,797 --> 00:05:55,635
That's why GPUs, originally built to colour in pixels for video games,

94
00:05:55,635 --> 00:05:58,068
became the engines of AI.

95
00:05:58,068 --> 00:06:02,447
The mathematics of neural networks is, almost perfectly, parallel.

96
00:06:04,263 --> 00:06:06,551
But here's the thing most people miss.

97
00:06:06,551 --> 00:06:09,166
Doing the arithmetic is only half the job.

98
00:06:09,166 --> 00:06:12,108
The numbers have to get to the cores first.

99
00:06:12,530 --> 00:06:14,235
The weights live in memory.

100
00:06:14,235 --> 00:06:18,328
Every time you use them, they have to travel through a pipe

101
00:06:18,328 --> 00:06:21,739
into the compute units. And that pipe has a width.

102
00:06:21,739 --> 00:06:24,468
Bandwidth. How many bytes per second can flow.

103
00:06:24,897 --> 00:06:29,360
If the cores can eat numbers faster than the pipe can deliver

104
00:06:29,360 --> 00:06:31,220
them, the cores sit idle.

105
00:06:31,220 --> 00:06:35,683
Waiting. And a waiting GPU is the most expensive way to do

106
00:06:35,683 --> 00:06:36,798
nothing ever invented.

107
00:06:37,330 --> 00:06:39,588
So there are really three walls.

108
00:06:39,588 --> 00:06:41,846
Compute: how fast you can multiply.

109
00:06:41,846 --> 00:06:46,362
Memory: how much you can hold, and how fast you can read

110
00:06:46,362 --> 00:06:50,878
it. And communication: how fast separate chips can talk to each other.

111
00:06:51,297 --> 00:06:53,182
Keep these three walls in mind.

112
00:06:53,182 --> 00:06:56,952
Every clever idea in the rest of this story is an attack

113
00:06:56,952 --> 00:06:58,209
on one of them.

114
00:06:59,663 --> 00:07:04,336
Now, a model the size of V3 doesn't fit on one GPU.

115
00:07:04,336 --> 00:07:09,009
Even stored at just one byte per parameter, its weights would need

116
00:07:09,009 --> 00:07:13,682
more than eight GPUs' worth of fast memory, before you've done anything

117
00:07:13,682 --> 00:07:14,460
at all.

118
00:07:14,863 --> 00:07:20,055
So the model gets split across many GPUs, and those GPUs have

119
00:07:20,055 --> 00:07:25,246
to constantly exchange numbers. The connections between them become part of the

120
00:07:25,246 --> 00:07:25,678
computer.

121
00:07:26,097 --> 00:07:30,238
And this is exactly where the export rules bit.

122
00:07:30,238 --> 00:07:34,840
The H800 kept essentially the same engine as the H100.

123
00:07:34,840 --> 00:07:38,522
What got cut was the pipe between chips.

124
00:07:38,522 --> 00:07:43,584
Widely reported as about 400 gigabytes per second, instead of 900.

125
00:07:44,097 --> 00:07:48,361
Same engine. Narrower pipes. Remember that, because it's going to matter much

126
00:07:48,361 --> 00:07:50,493
more than it seems right now.

127
00:07:50,493 --> 00:07:53,692
And it raises a question we'll come back to.

128
00:07:53,692 --> 00:07:57,601
If you can just add more chips, why isn't that enough?

129
00:07:59,630 --> 00:08:01,463
So we know the hardware.

130
00:08:01,463 --> 00:08:04,395
But what is all that multiplication actually for?

131
00:08:04,395 --> 00:08:07,327
Let's open up a language model and look.

132
00:08:08,097 --> 00:08:12,321
Here's a sentence. The animal didn't cross the street because it was

133
00:08:12,321 --> 00:08:12,673
tired.

134
00:08:13,297 --> 00:08:15,244
Question. What does it refer to?

135
00:08:15,244 --> 00:08:16,867
The animal, or the street?

136
00:08:17,697 --> 00:08:19,458
You knew instantly. The animal.

137
00:08:19,458 --> 00:08:22,628
Streets don't get tired. But notice how you knew.

138
00:08:22,628 --> 00:08:26,855
You looked at other words in the sentence and pulled their meaning

139
00:08:26,855 --> 00:08:27,911
into this one.

140
00:08:28,330 --> 00:08:32,781
A model has to do the same thing, with nothing but numbers.

141
00:08:32,781 --> 00:08:35,007
So first, the text becomes pieces.

142
00:08:35,007 --> 00:08:35,378
Tokens.

143
00:08:36,997 --> 00:08:40,247
Each token is then swapped for a list of numbers.

144
00:08:40,247 --> 00:08:42,522
A vector. We call it an embedding.

145
00:08:42,930 --> 00:08:45,608
Real models use thousands of numbers per token.

146
00:08:45,608 --> 00:08:49,624
We'll draw just two, so we can see them as an arrow

147
00:08:49,624 --> 00:08:50,628
on a page.

148
00:08:50,930 --> 00:08:56,275
These numbers are learned. During training, words that behave alike drift near

149
00:08:56,275 --> 00:08:58,948
each other. Animal lands near dog.

150
00:08:58,948 --> 00:09:02,511
Street lands near road. Tired lands near sleepy.

151
00:09:03,030 --> 00:09:05,606
Meaning, in a model, is a direction.

152
00:09:05,606 --> 00:09:10,023
And that means we can measure how related two words are with

153
00:09:10,023 --> 00:09:11,863
the operation we already know.

154
00:09:11,863 --> 00:09:12,968
The dot product.

155
00:09:14,796 --> 00:09:18,961
Here's the trick at the heart of every modern language model.

156
00:09:18,961 --> 00:09:23,504
Each token makes three new vectors from its embedding, each by multiplying

157
00:09:23,504 --> 00:09:25,397
with its own learned matrix.

158
00:09:25,829 --> 00:09:28,078
A query. What am I looking for?

159
00:09:28,078 --> 00:09:31,291
For it, something like: which noun am I talking about?

160
00:09:31,596 --> 00:09:34,448
A key. What do I have to offer?

161
00:09:34,448 --> 00:09:38,726
Every token advertises itself. Animal says: I'm a living thing, a noun.

162
00:09:39,029 --> 00:09:43,155
And a value. If you do pay attention to me, here's the

163
00:09:43,155 --> 00:09:44,531
information I'll hand over.

164
00:09:44,963 --> 00:09:48,783
To decide where it should look, we take its query, and dot

165
00:09:48,783 --> 00:09:51,648
it with the key of every word so far.

166
00:09:51,648 --> 00:09:55,150
A model that writes left to right can only look back.

167
00:09:55,150 --> 00:09:57,379
A big result means a good match.

168
00:09:57,796 --> 00:10:02,656
Do this for every token at once, and those dot products form

169
00:10:02,656 --> 00:10:05,085
a grid. Query times key transpose.

170
00:10:05,085 --> 00:10:06,300
Q K transpose.

171
00:10:08,028 --> 00:10:09,892
But raw scores are awkward.

172
00:10:09,892 --> 00:10:14,366
They can be negative, and they don't add up to anything in

173
00:10:14,366 --> 00:10:18,094
particular. We want percentages. How much attention should go where.

174
00:10:18,495 --> 00:10:20,466
So we do two things.

175
00:10:20,466 --> 00:10:24,014
First, raise e to the power of each score.

176
00:10:24,014 --> 00:10:27,561
Everything becomes positive, and big scores become much bigger.

177
00:10:27,961 --> 00:10:30,068
Then divide each by the total.

178
00:10:30,068 --> 00:10:32,175
Now they add up to one.

179
00:10:32,175 --> 00:10:33,579
This is called softmax.

180
00:10:33,995 --> 00:10:38,679
One more small detail. Before the softmax, the scores are divided by

181
00:10:38,679 --> 00:10:41,412
the square root of the vector length.

182
00:10:41,412 --> 00:10:46,096
Long vectors produce huge dot products, and this keeps the softmax from

183
00:10:46,096 --> 00:10:48,048
becoming too sharp to learn.

184
00:10:48,461 --> 00:10:52,446
And here's the result. It sends most of its attention to animal.

185
00:10:52,446 --> 00:10:55,435
The model has worked out what you worked out.

186
00:10:57,061 --> 00:10:58,905
Now it collects its reward.

187
00:10:58,905 --> 00:11:03,328
Take each token's value vector, scale it by its attention weight, and

188
00:11:03,328 --> 00:11:04,802
add them all up.

189
00:11:05,228 --> 00:11:08,332
The new vector for it is no longer just it.

190
00:11:08,332 --> 00:11:10,815
It now carries a large dose of animal.

191
00:11:11,228 --> 00:11:14,832
Put together, the whole mechanism fits on one line.

192
00:11:14,832 --> 00:11:19,637
Attention of Q, K and V equals softmax of Q K transpose

193
00:11:19,637 --> 00:11:21,639
over root d, times V.

194
00:11:22,261 --> 00:11:24,272
Look at it again, though.

195
00:11:24,272 --> 00:11:27,892
Three matrix multiplications to make Q, K and V.

196
00:11:27,892 --> 00:11:30,305
One to compare everything with everything.

197
00:11:30,305 --> 00:11:32,718
One more to blend the values.

198
00:11:32,718 --> 00:11:37,142
It's our old friend, multiply and add, all the way down.

199
00:11:38,961 --> 00:11:43,690
One attention pattern can only capture one kind of relationship at a

200
00:11:43,690 --> 00:11:48,419
time. So models run many in parallel, each with its own Q,

201
00:11:48,419 --> 00:11:51,177
K and V matrices. They're called heads.

202
00:11:51,595 --> 00:11:53,775
One head might track pronouns.

203
00:11:53,775 --> 00:11:56,392
Another, which word came just before.

204
00:11:56,392 --> 00:11:59,881
Another, subject and verb. Nobody tells them to.

205
00:11:59,881 --> 00:12:02,062
They specialise on their own.

206
00:12:02,461 --> 00:12:05,903
After attention comes the second half of each block.

207
00:12:05,903 --> 00:12:10,492
A feed-forward network. Two big matrices applied to each token on its

208
00:12:10,492 --> 00:12:15,080
own. If attention is where tokens talk to each other, this is

209
00:12:15,080 --> 00:12:17,375
where each token thinks it over.

210
00:12:17,795 --> 00:12:23,074
And in large models, most of the parameters live right here, in

211
00:12:23,074 --> 00:12:25,274
these feed-forward matrices. Remember that.

212
00:12:25,274 --> 00:12:27,473
It's about to become important.

213
00:12:29,295 --> 00:12:31,989
Attention, then feed-forward. That's one block.

214
00:12:31,989 --> 00:12:34,683
Now stack them. Dozens of times.

215
00:12:34,995 --> 00:12:39,843
At the top, the final vector is compared against every token in

216
00:12:39,843 --> 00:12:44,690
the vocabulary, and turned, with softmax again, into a probability for what

217
00:12:44,690 --> 00:12:45,498
comes next.

218
00:12:45,928 --> 00:12:48,318
Pick one. Add it to the text.

219
00:12:48,318 --> 00:12:50,025
Run the whole thing again.

220
00:12:50,025 --> 00:12:52,756
That's how every chatbot you've ever used writes.

221
00:12:52,756 --> 00:12:54,463
One token at a time.

222
00:12:54,895 --> 00:12:58,558
That's a Transformer. And now we can see the problem coming.

223
00:13:00,561 --> 00:13:03,046
Researchers kept discovering the same thing.

224
00:13:03,046 --> 00:13:08,015
Make the model bigger, train it on more text, and it gets

225
00:13:08,015 --> 00:13:09,671
better. Smoothly, predictably, better.

226
00:13:10,095 --> 00:13:12,204
So they made them bigger.

227
00:13:12,204 --> 00:13:14,735
More layers. Wider matrices. More heads.

228
00:13:14,735 --> 00:13:18,531
And every one of those multiplies the parameter count.

229
00:13:18,861 --> 00:13:21,354
But remember our rule of thumb.

230
00:13:21,354 --> 00:13:23,846
Two operations per parameter, per token.

231
00:13:23,846 --> 00:13:28,831
In an ordinary, dense model, every single token has to pass through

232
00:13:28,831 --> 00:13:30,077
every single weight.

233
00:13:30,495 --> 00:13:33,936
Double the model, double the cost of every word.

234
00:13:33,936 --> 00:13:35,465
Forever. For every user.

235
00:13:37,295 --> 00:13:39,929
Now think about what that means in practice.

236
00:13:39,929 --> 00:13:42,893
You ask a giant model, what's two plus two.

237
00:13:43,295 --> 00:13:46,799
And to answer, it fires every weight it has.

238
00:13:46,799 --> 00:13:51,471
Including the ones that learned French poetry, and medieval history, and how

239
00:13:51,471 --> 00:13:53,418
to write a SQL query.

240
00:13:53,828 --> 00:13:58,638
That seems wasteful. When you do arithmetic, you don't consult everything you

241
00:13:58,638 --> 00:13:59,841
know about poetry.

242
00:14:00,361 --> 00:14:02,939
So here's the question that changes everything.

243
00:14:02,939 --> 00:14:07,359
What if a model didn't need to use all of itself, every

244
00:14:07,359 --> 00:14:08,095
single time?

245
00:14:10,328 --> 00:14:14,333
Go back to the feed-forward network, the part of each block where

246
00:14:14,333 --> 00:14:16,002
most of the parameters live.

247
00:14:16,328 --> 00:14:21,123
Now, instead of one huge feed-forward network, cut it into several smaller

248
00:14:21,123 --> 00:14:25,119
ones. Each is a complete little network on its own.

249
00:14:25,119 --> 00:14:26,717
We'll call them experts.

250
00:14:27,128 --> 00:14:30,954
Here's the key idea. For any given token, we only run a

251
00:14:30,954 --> 00:14:33,505
few of them. The rest stay switched off.

252
00:14:33,928 --> 00:14:37,556
The model can hold the knowledge of all eight experts.

253
00:14:37,556 --> 00:14:40,095
But each token only pays for two.

254
00:14:40,095 --> 00:14:42,634
This is called a Mixture of Experts.

255
00:14:44,461 --> 00:14:46,557
Which raises an obvious question.

256
00:14:46,557 --> 00:14:49,911
Who decides which experts a token should visit?

257
00:14:50,328 --> 00:14:52,723
Another small learned piece of the network.

258
00:14:52,723 --> 00:14:56,827
A router. It looks at the token and gives every expert a

259
00:14:56,827 --> 00:14:57,169
score.

260
00:14:57,595 --> 00:14:59,941
Watch what happens with different tokens.

261
00:14:59,941 --> 00:15:03,459
The word integral lights up one pair of experts.

262
00:15:03,761 --> 00:15:07,101
The code word def lights up a different pair.

263
00:15:07,101 --> 00:15:11,553
The word mitochondria, another. Even a word like the gets its own

264
00:15:11,553 --> 00:15:11,924
routing.

265
00:15:12,428 --> 00:15:18,360
Nobody hand-assigns topics. The router and the experts learn together, and the

266
00:15:18,360 --> 00:15:24,292
specialisation emerges from training. Real experts rarely map to tidy human subjects.

267
00:15:24,292 --> 00:15:26,764
We're simplifying for the picture.

268
00:15:28,595 --> 00:15:32,262
In symbols, it's short. The router is just a matrix.

269
00:15:32,262 --> 00:15:36,662
Multiply the token's vector by it, and you get one score per

270
00:15:36,662 --> 00:15:37,029
expert.

271
00:15:37,461 --> 00:15:41,542
Turn the scores into weights, keep only the top k, and throw

272
00:15:41,542 --> 00:15:43,923
the rest away. That's top k routing.

273
00:15:44,328 --> 00:15:49,207
The output is the chosen experts' answers, blended by their weights.

274
00:15:49,207 --> 00:15:54,530
The same weighted-sum trick as attention, just applied to experts instead of

275
00:15:54,530 --> 00:15:54,973
words.

276
00:15:55,395 --> 00:15:59,769
But there's a trap. If the router falls in love with one

277
00:15:59,769 --> 00:16:03,778
expert, every token piles into it, while the others sit idle.

278
00:16:03,778 --> 00:16:07,787
You'd have a giant model, secretly behaving like a small one.

279
00:16:08,195 --> 00:16:11,010
So training has to balance the load.

280
00:16:11,010 --> 00:16:15,836
DeepSeek does it by nudging each expert's score up or down, depending

281
00:16:15,836 --> 00:16:18,249
on how busy it has been.

282
00:16:18,249 --> 00:16:23,075
In V4.1 Flash, they even keep separate nudges for text and for

283
00:16:23,075 --> 00:16:23,477
images.

284
00:16:25,294 --> 00:16:28,201
DeepSeek pushed this idea further than most.

285
00:16:28,201 --> 00:16:33,184
Two changes. First, lots of small experts instead of a few big

286
00:16:33,184 --> 00:16:36,921
ones, so tokens can mix and match more precisely.

287
00:16:37,228 --> 00:16:42,070
Second, one shared expert that every token always visits, to hold common

288
00:16:42,070 --> 00:16:46,508
knowledge, so the specialists don't all have to relearn the basics.

289
00:16:46,928 --> 00:16:53,759
DeepSeek V2 had 236 billion parameters, but only about 21 billion active

290
00:16:53,759 --> 00:16:54,897
per token.

291
00:16:55,228 --> 00:17:01,412
V3 went further. In each of its expert layers, 256 routed experts

292
00:17:01,412 --> 00:17:07,596
plus one shared expert. Each token visits just 8 of the 256.

293
00:17:07,928 --> 00:17:13,491
671 billion parameters stored. 37 billion used for any one token.

294
00:17:13,491 --> 00:17:16,525
About five and a half percent.

295
00:17:17,028 --> 00:17:24,328
And the 2026 model, V4.1 Flash, uses 384 routed experts per layer,

296
00:17:24,328 --> 00:17:27,978
with 6 chosen for each token.

297
00:17:28,394 --> 00:17:32,702
So a model can be enormous, and still cheap to run, per

298
00:17:32,702 --> 00:17:34,497
token. That answers our question.

299
00:17:34,497 --> 00:17:37,009
But it creates a brand new one.

300
00:17:37,009 --> 00:17:39,522
Where do all these experts actually live?

301
00:17:41,528 --> 00:17:46,468
Remember, a model like V3 is far too big for one GPU.

302
00:17:46,468 --> 00:17:48,939
So the experts are spread out.

303
00:17:48,939 --> 00:17:53,879
A few here, a few there, across many GPUs, across many machines.

304
00:17:54,294 --> 00:17:56,692
Now follow a single token.

305
00:17:56,692 --> 00:18:02,445
The router says: you need expert 12, expert 97, and expert 200.

306
00:18:02,445 --> 00:18:05,322
And they're on three different GPUs.

307
00:18:05,728 --> 00:18:09,170
So the token's vector has to be sent there.

308
00:18:09,170 --> 00:18:11,464
Computed. And the results sent back.

309
00:18:11,464 --> 00:18:12,612
Dispatch, then combine.

310
00:18:12,928 --> 00:18:17,769
Now do that for every token in a batch, at every expert

311
00:18:17,769 --> 00:18:22,609
layer. Every GPU is sending to every other GPU, all at once.

312
00:18:22,609 --> 00:18:25,433
Engineers call it all to all communication.

313
00:18:27,261 --> 00:18:30,696
Let's draw what one GPU's time looks like.

314
00:18:30,696 --> 00:18:33,702
Compute. Then wait for tokens to arrive.

315
00:18:33,702 --> 00:18:36,708
Compute. Wait for results to come back.

316
00:18:37,128 --> 00:18:41,477
Those grey gaps are the GPU doing nothing, while its numbers are

317
00:18:41,477 --> 00:18:45,827
stuck in the wires. And the narrower the wire, the longer the

318
00:18:45,827 --> 00:18:46,190
gaps.

319
00:18:46,594 --> 00:18:51,477
Now you can see why the H800's narrower links mattered.

320
00:18:51,477 --> 00:18:54,895
MoE saves compute, but it spends communication.

321
00:18:54,895 --> 00:19:00,266
And communication was exactly the resource the export rules had squeezed.

322
00:19:00,694 --> 00:19:03,645
That's the answer to the question from earlier.

323
00:19:03,645 --> 00:19:05,488
Why aren't more GPUs enough?

324
00:19:05,488 --> 00:19:09,913
Because every GPU you add is another island, and islands have to

325
00:19:09,913 --> 00:19:14,339
talk. Past a point, you're not limited by how fast you can

326
00:19:14,339 --> 00:19:17,657
think. You're limited by how fast you can talk.

327
00:19:19,661 --> 00:19:22,912
DeepSeek's answer for V3 wasn't a faster wire.

328
00:19:22,912 --> 00:19:24,943
It was a smarter schedule.

329
00:19:25,361 --> 00:19:29,157
The idea is simple to say and very hard to build.

330
00:19:29,157 --> 00:19:33,298
While one batch of tokens is travelling, compute on a different batch.

331
00:19:33,298 --> 00:19:37,094
Keep the engine busy while the mail is in the post.

332
00:19:37,494 --> 00:19:43,827
They called their pipeline schedule DualPipe, and wrote custom communication code so

333
00:19:43,827 --> 00:19:47,521
that computation and communication overlap almost completely.

334
00:19:47,928 --> 00:19:51,250
Then they attacked the size of the mail itself.

335
00:19:51,250 --> 00:19:53,835
Numbers in a computer take up space.

336
00:19:53,835 --> 00:19:57,157
A common format uses 16 bits for each one.

337
00:19:57,157 --> 00:20:01,218
V3 trained with much of its maths in just 8 bits.

338
00:20:01,628 --> 00:20:05,784
Half the bits means half the bytes to store, half the bytes

339
00:20:05,784 --> 00:20:09,247
to move, and faster arithmetic on hardware built for it.

340
00:20:09,247 --> 00:20:13,403
DeepSeek says V3 was the first to prove this works at such

341
00:20:13,403 --> 00:20:14,096
a scale.

342
00:20:14,628 --> 00:20:16,622
It's a pattern worth noticing.

343
00:20:16,622 --> 00:20:21,408
Not more hardware. A better understanding of where the time was actually

344
00:20:21,408 --> 00:20:21,807
going.

345
00:20:24,028 --> 00:20:28,062
There's one wall left. And it's the one that shows up when

346
00:20:28,062 --> 00:20:29,743
you actually use these models.

347
00:20:29,743 --> 00:20:30,080
Memory.

348
00:20:30,594 --> 00:20:32,494
Remember how a model writes.

349
00:20:32,494 --> 00:20:34,394
One token at a time.

350
00:20:34,394 --> 00:20:38,954
Each new token looks back, with attention, at every token before it.

351
00:20:39,361 --> 00:20:43,237
To do that, it needs every earlier token's key and value.

352
00:20:43,237 --> 00:20:47,465
Now, those don't change. The key for animal is the same at

353
00:20:47,465 --> 00:20:50,284
word ten as it was at word five.

354
00:20:50,694 --> 00:20:55,244
So instead of recomputing them for every new word, the model saves

355
00:20:55,244 --> 00:20:59,793
them. A shelf of keys and values that grows by one entry

356
00:20:59,793 --> 00:21:01,688
per token. The KV cache.

357
00:21:03,494 --> 00:21:08,096
Let's measure the shelf. For ordinary attention, each token stores a key

358
00:21:08,096 --> 00:21:11,547
and a value, for every head, in every layer.

359
00:21:11,961 --> 00:21:18,164
Here's a hypothetical. If a model with V3's shape had used ordinary

360
00:21:18,164 --> 00:21:24,366
attention, with 128 heads of 128 numbers, over 61 layers, that's about

361
00:21:24,366 --> 00:21:27,467
4 megabytes, for every single token.

362
00:21:27,894 --> 00:21:33,077
Now a long conversation. A hundred and twenty eight thousand tokens.

363
00:21:33,077 --> 00:21:35,433
That's over five hundred gigabytes.

364
00:21:35,433 --> 00:21:36,847
For one conversation.

365
00:21:37,361 --> 00:21:40,011
The weights are shared by every user.

366
00:21:40,011 --> 00:21:41,903
But this cache is personal.

367
00:21:41,903 --> 00:21:43,796
Every conversation carries its own.

368
00:21:43,796 --> 00:21:48,338
That's what fills the memory of a GPU during serving, and it's

369
00:21:48,338 --> 00:21:52,502
what limits how many people one machine can serve at once.

370
00:21:52,928 --> 00:21:57,128
So the question becomes: how small can a memory of the past

371
00:21:57,128 --> 00:21:57,828
possibly be?

372
00:22:00,160 --> 00:22:05,359
DeepSeek's first big answer, back in V2, was called Multi-head Latent Attention.

373
00:22:05,359 --> 00:22:06,658
M L A.

374
00:22:07,060 --> 00:22:11,679
Here's the intuition. All those keys and values, across all the heads,

375
00:22:11,679 --> 00:22:13,989
are made from the same token.

376
00:22:13,989 --> 00:22:18,608
They're highly redundant. So instead of storing them all, store one small

377
00:22:18,608 --> 00:22:23,227
compressed summary, a latent vector, and rebuild the keys and values from

378
00:22:23,227 --> 00:22:25,151
it when you need them.

379
00:22:25,560 --> 00:22:28,904
The rebuild is just another matrix multiplication.

380
00:22:28,904 --> 00:22:33,203
And multiplication is the thing GPUs have in abundance.

381
00:22:33,203 --> 00:22:36,547
You're trading cheap compute for expensive memory.

382
00:22:36,960 --> 00:22:44,362
In V3's published configuration, each token stores a 512 number summary, plus

383
00:22:44,362 --> 00:22:51,147
64 numbers for position. 576 numbers, instead of roughly 32 thousand.

384
00:22:51,560 --> 00:22:57,414
When they introduced it in V2, DeepSeek reported a 93.3 percent cut

385
00:22:57,414 --> 00:23:02,779
in KV cache compared with their earlier 67 billion parameter model.

386
00:23:04,594 --> 00:23:09,171
Now step back. The size of the cache is really three numbers

387
00:23:09,171 --> 00:23:09,934
multiplied together.

388
00:23:10,360 --> 00:23:12,143
How big each entry is.

389
00:23:12,143 --> 00:23:14,995
How many entries you keep along the sequence.

390
00:23:14,995 --> 00:23:17,491
And how many layers keep their own.

391
00:23:17,894 --> 00:23:19,851
MLA turned the first dial.

392
00:23:19,851 --> 00:23:24,550
Make each entry smaller. But the other two dials were still there,

393
00:23:24,550 --> 00:23:24,941
untouched.

394
00:23:25,360 --> 00:23:29,671
And by 2026, a new kind of workload was about to make

395
00:23:29,671 --> 00:23:32,544
those two dials the only ones that mattered.

396
00:23:34,560 --> 00:23:38,001
By 2026, people weren't just chatting with models.

397
00:23:38,001 --> 00:23:40,151
They were handing them jobs.

398
00:23:40,151 --> 00:23:43,161
Read this code base. Run the tests.

399
00:23:43,161 --> 00:23:45,311
Fix the bug. Try again.

400
00:23:45,727 --> 00:23:50,458
Every tool call pours more text back into the context.

401
00:23:50,458 --> 00:23:54,243
The inputs grow enormous, the outputs stay small.

402
00:23:54,243 --> 00:23:58,028
DeepSeek describes these workloads as increasingly input heavy.

403
00:23:58,460 --> 00:24:05,176
In September 2026, DeepSeek published a paper about exactly this, for a

404
00:24:05,176 --> 00:24:07,974
model called DeepSeek V4.1 Flash.

405
00:24:07,974 --> 00:24:14,689
552 billion backbone parameters, and contexts of up to a million tokens.

406
00:24:15,094 --> 00:24:18,354
And the paper is candid about the bottleneck.

407
00:24:18,354 --> 00:24:23,245
Earlier sparse attention had already tamed much of the compute cost of

408
00:24:23,245 --> 00:24:28,136
long contexts, which left storage and data movement as the prominent bottlenecks.

409
00:24:28,136 --> 00:24:29,359
The KV cache.

410
00:24:31,160 --> 00:24:35,415
First, the sequence dial. When a new token is written, does it

411
00:24:35,415 --> 00:24:38,606
really need to look at all million earlier tokens?

412
00:24:39,027 --> 00:24:44,964
V4.1 Flash says no. A small, cheap scorer, called an indexer, quickly

413
00:24:44,964 --> 00:24:50,902
rates the past entries, and the model attends properly only to the

414
00:24:50,902 --> 00:24:51,891
top 512.

415
00:24:52,294 --> 00:24:57,995
Early layers also compress the past, merging neighbouring tokens into shared entries.

416
00:24:57,995 --> 00:25:03,696
And every layer keeps a short, precise window of the most recent

417
00:25:03,696 --> 00:25:07,022
128 tokens, so nothing nearby gets lost.

418
00:25:07,427 --> 00:25:11,040
Remember the grid of every token compared with every other?

419
00:25:11,040 --> 00:25:13,569
Double the text and that grid quadruples.

420
00:25:13,569 --> 00:25:17,905
Picking a fixed number of entries keeps each new token's work on

421
00:25:17,905 --> 00:25:18,627
a leash.

422
00:25:20,460 --> 00:25:25,473
Now the layer dial. Traditionally, every layer keeps its own shelf of

423
00:25:25,473 --> 00:25:28,396
keys and values. Forty layers, forty shelves.

424
00:25:28,827 --> 00:25:33,814
V4.1 Flash introduces what it calls Compressed Sparse Attention 2.

425
00:25:33,814 --> 00:25:36,806
Its layers come in three modes.

426
00:25:37,127 --> 00:25:41,789
A Full layer builds the shared memory and picks which entries matter.

427
00:25:41,789 --> 00:25:46,452
A Reindex layer borrows that memory, but re-scores it with its own

428
00:25:46,452 --> 00:25:51,114
question. And a Reuse layer borrows both, the memory and the selection.

429
00:25:51,114 --> 00:25:53,445
It keeps only its own query.

430
00:25:53,860 --> 00:25:57,453
In the encoder, layers come in groups of six.

431
00:25:57,453 --> 00:25:59,449
One Full, then five Reuse.

432
00:25:59,449 --> 00:26:01,844
In the decoder, groups of four.

433
00:26:01,844 --> 00:26:05,037
Most layers stop storing their own shelf entirely.

434
00:26:05,460 --> 00:26:08,513
The trade is the same one MLA made.

435
00:26:08,513 --> 00:26:11,947
A little more computation, and a lot less memory.

436
00:26:11,947 --> 00:26:16,526
And DeepSeek is upfront that it has a cost: the shared selection

437
00:26:16,526 --> 00:26:18,815
can occasionally pick the wrong entries.

438
00:26:20,627 --> 00:26:22,928
Then the entry size dial again.

439
00:26:22,928 --> 00:26:27,528
This time not by storing fewer numbers, but by storing each number

440
00:26:27,528 --> 00:26:28,679
with fewer bits.

441
00:26:29,094 --> 00:26:31,770
Four bits give you only sixteen patterns.

442
00:26:31,770 --> 00:26:36,359
In the format DeepSeek uses, they stand for: zero, a half, one,

443
00:26:36,359 --> 00:26:40,565
one and a half, two, three, four, six, and their negatives.

444
00:26:40,994 --> 00:26:42,803
That's a very coarse ruler.

445
00:26:42,803 --> 00:26:47,145
The trick is to give every group of 16 numbers its own

446
00:26:47,145 --> 00:26:51,487
shared scale, so the ruler stretches to fit whatever range that group

447
00:26:51,487 --> 00:26:51,849
needs.

448
00:26:52,260 --> 00:26:56,989
DeepSeek stores the main cache this way, and keeps the more sensitive

449
00:26:56,989 --> 00:26:58,959
local window in 8 bits.

450
00:26:58,959 --> 00:27:03,687
Compared with the 8 bit main cache of V4, that nearly halves

451
00:27:03,687 --> 00:27:04,475
its storage.

452
00:27:06,294 --> 00:27:11,047
One more idea, aimed at the moment a long prompt first arrives.

453
00:27:11,047 --> 00:27:15,799
This is called prefill, reading the input, as opposed to decode, writing

454
00:27:15,799 --> 00:27:16,592
the output.

455
00:27:16,994 --> 00:27:22,164
V4.1 Flash splits its 40 layers into a 20 layer encoder and

456
00:27:22,164 --> 00:27:27,334
a 20 layer decoder. The upper half doesn't compute its long-range memory

457
00:27:27,334 --> 00:27:32,504
from its own layers. It projects it directly from the encoder's final

458
00:27:32,504 --> 00:27:32,935
output.

459
00:27:33,360 --> 00:27:37,851
So when a long prompt arrives, only the bottom half has to

460
00:27:37,851 --> 00:27:42,342
process all of it. The paper says this nearly halves prefill computation.

461
00:27:42,760 --> 00:27:47,697
Which is why the paper reports about 8 billion active parameters per

462
00:27:47,697 --> 00:27:51,399
token when reading, and about 16 billion when writing.

463
00:27:53,227 --> 00:27:55,379
Now put the dials together.

464
00:27:55,379 --> 00:27:58,392
Here's a chart from DeepSeek's own paper.

465
00:27:58,392 --> 00:28:03,126
The global KV cache per token, across generations of their models.

466
00:28:03,527 --> 00:28:10,379
Their first model, in 2023: about 389 thousand bytes per token.

467
00:28:10,694 --> 00:28:16,513
V3.2, at the end of 2025: about 48 thousand.

468
00:28:16,827 --> 00:28:23,227
V4 Flash, in April 2026: 3,514.

469
00:28:23,527 --> 00:28:27,386
And V4.1 Flash: 890 bytes.

470
00:28:28,394 --> 00:28:30,796
That's the number from the start.

471
00:28:30,796 --> 00:28:35,602
About 437 times smaller than their first model, and roughly a quarter

472
00:28:35,602 --> 00:28:38,004
of the model just before it.

473
00:28:38,004 --> 00:28:41,208
Less than a kilobyte, to remember each token.

474
00:28:41,727 --> 00:28:46,991
And one more result. Stretching the context 256 times, from 4 thousand

475
00:28:46,991 --> 00:28:52,254
tokens to a million, raises the compute for each new token by

476
00:28:52,254 --> 00:28:54,009
only about a quarter.

477
00:28:54,427 --> 00:28:59,827
The paper also reports that, despite this far smaller memory, V4.1 Flash

478
00:28:59,827 --> 00:29:03,426
performs substantially better than the model before it.

479
00:29:03,426 --> 00:29:06,576
That's DeepSeek's claim, on their own benchmarks.

480
00:29:06,576 --> 00:29:09,276
But it's the claim that matters.

481
00:29:09,276 --> 00:29:11,975
They didn't trade quality for memory.

482
00:29:14,094 --> 00:29:16,024
We've talked about running a model.

483
00:29:16,024 --> 00:29:19,885
But where do all those billions of weights come from in the

484
00:29:19,885 --> 00:29:22,137
first place? Nobody writes them by hand.

485
00:29:22,560 --> 00:29:24,340
They start as random numbers.

486
00:29:24,340 --> 00:29:28,610
And a model with random weights, asked to continue the cat sat

487
00:29:28,610 --> 00:29:30,390
on the, will guess nonsense.

488
00:29:30,794 --> 00:29:32,858
But we know the right answer.

489
00:29:32,858 --> 00:29:36,298
Mat. So we can measure how wrong the model was.

490
00:29:36,298 --> 00:29:40,426
The standard measure is the negative log of the probability it gave

491
00:29:40,426 --> 00:29:42,834
the right answer. It's called the loss.

492
00:29:43,260 --> 00:29:46,451
Confident and right, the loss is near zero.

493
00:29:46,451 --> 00:29:48,446
Confident and wrong, it's huge.

494
00:29:48,446 --> 00:29:52,434
Now the entire goal of training fits in one sentence.

495
00:29:52,434 --> 00:29:54,030
Make that number smaller.

496
00:29:55,860 --> 00:29:59,607
Picture just one weight. Change it a little, and the loss goes

497
00:29:59,607 --> 00:30:02,729
up or down. Plot that, and you get a landscape.

498
00:30:03,160 --> 00:30:07,270
The slope at our current position tells us which way is downhill.

499
00:30:07,270 --> 00:30:09,325
That slope is called the gradient.

500
00:30:09,727 --> 00:30:12,534
So we take a small step downhill.

501
00:30:12,534 --> 00:30:17,348
New weight equals old weight, minus a small step size times the

502
00:30:17,348 --> 00:30:19,754
gradient. Then measure again. Step again.

503
00:30:20,160 --> 00:30:24,916
A real model does this for every one of its weights at

504
00:30:24,916 --> 00:30:28,087
once, in a landscape with billions of dimensions.

505
00:30:28,087 --> 00:30:32,842
The algorithm that finds all those slopes efficiently, by working backwards from

506
00:30:32,842 --> 00:30:36,013
the loss through every layer, is called backpropagation.

507
00:30:36,427 --> 00:30:41,116
And the backward pass costs roughly twice as much as the forward

508
00:30:41,116 --> 00:30:45,415
pass. So training costs about six operations per parameter, per token.

509
00:30:47,227 --> 00:30:49,107
So training is a loop.

510
00:30:49,107 --> 00:30:50,987
Take a batch of text.

511
00:30:50,987 --> 00:30:53,243
Run it forward. Measure the loss.

512
00:30:53,243 --> 00:30:55,123
Run backwards to get gradients.

513
00:30:55,123 --> 00:30:56,628
Nudge every weight. Repeat.

514
00:30:57,027 --> 00:31:02,240
How big a batch? For V4.1 Flash, the paper reports about a

515
00:31:02,240 --> 00:31:05,281
hundred million tokens in every single step.

516
00:31:05,593 --> 00:31:09,431
And it trained on 45 trillion tokens in total.

517
00:31:09,431 --> 00:31:14,548
Divide one by the other, and that's roughly 450 thousand turns of

518
00:31:14,548 --> 00:31:15,401
this loop.

519
00:31:15,827 --> 00:31:19,302
Every turn involving all the walls at once.

520
00:31:19,302 --> 00:31:22,342
Compute for the forward and backward passes.

521
00:31:22,342 --> 00:31:25,383
Memory for the weights and the optimizer.

522
00:31:25,383 --> 00:31:30,595
And communication, because thousands of GPUs have to agree on the update.

523
00:31:32,427 --> 00:31:37,604
Now we can read DeepSeek V3's training numbers with understanding, instead of

524
00:31:37,604 --> 00:31:38,035
awe.

525
00:31:38,460 --> 00:31:41,862
37 billion active parameters. 14.8 trillion tokens.

526
00:31:41,862 --> 00:31:47,694
Plug them into the rule of thumb, six times parameters times tokens,

527
00:31:47,694 --> 00:31:53,040
and you get roughly three times ten to the twenty-four operations.

528
00:31:53,040 --> 00:31:56,928
That's our estimate, not a figure from DeepSeek.

529
00:31:57,360 --> 00:32:04,550
DeepSeek reports that each trillion tokens took 180 thousand H800 GPU hours.

530
00:32:04,550 --> 00:32:11,141
About three point seven days, on their cluster of 2,048 GPUs.

531
00:32:11,560 --> 00:32:19,094
And the whole run, pre-training, context extension and post-training, added up to

532
00:32:19,094 --> 00:32:21,605
2.788 million GPU hours.

533
00:32:23,427 --> 00:32:28,062
Which brings us to the most quoted number in this whole story.

534
00:32:28,062 --> 00:32:29,607
About 5.6 million dollars.

535
00:32:30,027 --> 00:32:32,382
Here's where it comes from.

536
00:32:32,382 --> 00:32:38,035
DeepSeek took those 2.788 million GPU hours and assumed a rental price

537
00:32:38,035 --> 00:32:40,862
of two dollars per GPU hour.

538
00:32:40,862 --> 00:32:43,689
That's it. Hours times a price.

539
00:32:44,093 --> 00:32:48,714
And in the same report, they say plainly what it excludes.

540
00:32:48,714 --> 00:32:53,755
The costs of prior research and ablation experiments on architectures, algorithms and

541
00:32:53,755 --> 00:32:58,796
data. It also doesn't include buying the GPUs, the people, or the

542
00:32:58,796 --> 00:33:02,156
failed attempts that taught them what to build.

543
00:33:02,560 --> 00:33:07,808
So the honest headline was never that DeepSeek built a frontier model

544
00:33:07,808 --> 00:33:13,055
for 5.6 million dollars. It's that the final training run, by their

545
00:33:13,055 --> 00:33:14,804
accounting, was remarkably efficient.

546
00:33:15,327 --> 00:33:18,138
And some things are simply not public.

547
00:33:18,138 --> 00:33:22,956
The V4.1 Flash paper, for instance, doesn't say what hardware it was

548
00:33:22,956 --> 00:33:25,366
trained on, or what it cost.

549
00:33:25,366 --> 00:33:26,972
So we won't guess.

550
00:33:28,793 --> 00:33:31,475
So what actually was surprising?

551
00:33:31,475 --> 00:33:37,376
Not a secret invention. Mixture of experts, attention, low precision numbers.

552
00:33:37,376 --> 00:33:40,057
Those ideas existed before DeepSeek.

553
00:33:40,460 --> 00:33:44,469
What was surprising was how far they were pushed, and how deliberately

554
00:33:44,469 --> 00:33:47,141
each one was aimed at a specific wall.

555
00:33:47,560 --> 00:33:50,568
Compute: activate only the experts you need.

556
00:33:50,568 --> 00:33:54,006
Communication: overlap the waiting and shrink the numbers.

557
00:33:54,006 --> 00:33:59,162
Memory: compress the cache, share it across layers, store it in four

558
00:33:59,162 --> 00:33:59,592
bits.

559
00:33:59,993 --> 00:34:03,902
Each one, on its own, is a sensible engineering choice.

560
00:34:03,902 --> 00:34:08,201
Together, they add up to a system shaped around its bottlenecks.

561
00:34:08,201 --> 00:34:09,764
Not around its budget.

562
00:34:11,793 --> 00:34:15,334
Let's zoom out and look at the whole machine, one last time.

563
00:34:15,760 --> 00:34:18,238
Text becomes tokens. Tokens become vectors.

564
00:34:18,238 --> 00:34:21,542
Attention lets them borrow meaning from each other.

565
00:34:21,542 --> 00:34:25,673
A router sends each one to a handful of experts.

566
00:34:25,673 --> 00:34:29,390
The output becomes a guess about the next token.

567
00:34:29,793 --> 00:34:32,985
The guess is scored. The loss flows backwards.

568
00:34:32,985 --> 00:34:35,379
Every weight moves a tiny step.

569
00:34:35,379 --> 00:34:37,373
And the loop begins again.

570
00:34:37,373 --> 00:34:39,368
Hundreds of thousands of times.

571
00:34:39,793 --> 00:34:43,769
And over all of it, the three walls.

572
00:34:43,769 --> 00:34:47,744
Compute, wherever numbers multiply. Memory, wherever numbers wait.

573
00:34:47,744 --> 00:34:49,732
Communication, wherever numbers travel.

574
00:34:51,560 --> 00:34:53,756
So, back to the question we started with.

575
00:34:53,756 --> 00:34:57,051
What do you do when you can't simply buy your way out?

576
00:34:57,660 --> 00:35:01,633
You stop treating intelligence as a shopping problem, and start treating it

577
00:35:01,633 --> 00:35:04,613
as a physics problem. Where do the numbers go?

578
00:35:04,613 --> 00:35:06,269
How long do they wait?

579
00:35:06,269 --> 00:35:07,924
How far do they travel?

580
00:35:07,924 --> 00:35:10,242
How many bits do they really need?

581
00:35:10,760 --> 00:35:13,176
The constraint didn't make the problem easier.

582
00:35:13,176 --> 00:35:16,282
But it may have made understanding it more valuable.

583
00:35:16,282 --> 00:35:19,732
When you can't add more, you have to waste less.

584
00:35:20,560 --> 00:35:22,951
And that leaves one question open.

585
00:35:22,951 --> 00:35:27,732
Everything in this story was about fitting the mathematics to hardware that

586
00:35:27,732 --> 00:35:32,513
already existed. DeepSeek even chose its four-bit format so it would work

587
00:35:32,513 --> 00:35:34,506
across many kinds of chips.

588
00:35:35,027 --> 00:35:39,594
So what happens when the algorithms and the hardware start being designed

589
00:35:39,594 --> 00:35:42,259
together, each one shaped around the other?

590
00:35:43,260 --> 00:35:45,257
That's a story for another time.
